--TEST--
Core\IO::move asks `fs.write` for the path it takes away, so the exact pair of paths Core\IO::copy is granted for is refused for a move — the difference between the two doors, asserted as one comparison rather than two separate refusals — `rule:security/capability-question-is-grant-and-scope` and `rule:security/capability-check-at-the-door`, spec § 14
--FILE--
<?nvs
// One source, one destination, two members. `source` is readable and not
// writable, `scratch` is both; a copy is allowed and a move is not, because a
// move destroys the source and destruction is `fs.write` whatever the grant on
// the destination says. A door that reused `copy`'s pair of checks for `move`
// passes everything else written about either member and fails here.
// `Here::path` returns a literal resolved from this file's folder, so both
// paths held in variables are absolute.
class Here {
    public static function path(#[Core\Path] string $path): string {
        return $path;
    }
}
string $from = Here::path("source/note.txt");
string $to = Here::path("scratch/note.txt");

Core\IO::copy($from, $to);
echo "copy, with the source readable: it ran\n";

// Same two paths, and now the source is being taken away rather than read.
try {
    Core\IO::move($from, $to);
    echo "not reached\n";
} catch (RuntimeError $denied) {
    if ($denied->message == "Core\\IO::move needs the capability `fs.write` for " . $from . ", which is not granted\nhelp: grant it in nvs.toml under `[capabilities.fs]`") {
        echo "move, with the same two paths: refused, naming fs.write for the source\n";
    } else {
        echo "move, with the same two paths: refused, saying ", $denied->message, "\n";
    }
}

// The refusal is at the door, so the source is still where it was and the
// destination still holds what the copy put there.
if (Core\IO::exists($from)) {
    echo "the source survived the refusal\n";
} else {
    echo "the source was taken anyway\n";
}
if (Core\IO::read($to) == Core\IO::read($from)) {
    echo "the destination still holds the copy\n";
} else {
    echo "the destination changed\n";
}

// And inside the write grant on both ends, the same member runs: what refused
// above is the grant and not the member.
Core\IO::move($to, "scratch/moved.txt");
if (Core\IO::exists($to)) {
    echo "granted both ends: the old name is still there\n";
} else {
    echo "granted both ends: the old name is gone\n";
}
--FILE source/note.txt--
granted for reading
--FILE scratch/keep.txt--
the writable directory exists before the program runs
--FILE nvs.toml--
[capabilities.fs]
read = ["source", "scratch"]
write = ["scratch"]
--EXPECT--
copy, with the source readable: it ran
move, with the same two paths: refused, naming fs.write for the source
the source survived the refusal
the destination still holds the copy
granted both ends: the old name is gone
